      "name": null,
      "problem": "In $1990$, there were $5,000$ cell phone subscribers in a city. The number of subscribers increased by $35%$ per year until $2005.$ How many cell-phone subscribers were there in $2005?$",
      "worked_solution": "The number of subscribers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $35%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the growth rate ($35%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.35)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.35)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for the number of subscribers after $15$ years, we get\n\n$$\ny(15)=5,000\\cdot (1.35)^{15}≈450\\,792.\n$$\n\nWe round to the nearest whole number because we are talking about people.",
